+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration for
+deploying to a single Linux server. Everything runs in one container:
+
+- the Rails web server,
+- background jobs (Solid Queue runs inside Puma), and
+- SQLite databases plus uploaded files, stored in a named Docker volume.
+
+[Kamal's proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy) handles the public
+HTTP/HTTPS traffic and obtains a free Let's Encrypt SSL certificate for your
+hostname.
+
+## What you'll need
+
+- A Linux server with Docker and SSH access. Kamal can install Docker for you
+  the first time you run `kamal setup`.
+- A hostname whose DNS `A`/`AAAA` record points at that server. The server must
+  be reachable on ports 80 and 443 so the proxy can obtain a certificate.
+- An SMTP server for outbound email (magic links, notifications, exports).
+- Docker running on your local machine, which is used to build the image.
+- Kamal installed locally: `gem install kamal`, or use the `bin/kamal`
+  binstub from the repository.
+
+## Setup
+
+1. Fork and clone the repository, then install dependencies with `bin/setup`
+   (or at least `bundle install`).
+
+2. Create Rails credentials (only needed once). This writes the encrypted
+   `config/credentials.yml.enc` and a local `config/master.key`; commit the
+   encrypted file, but never commit `config/master.key`.
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/rails credentials:edit
+   ```
+
+   The generated credentials include a `secret_key_base`, which Rails uses in
+   production. Kamal passes `config/master.key` to the container as
+   `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` when deploying.
+
+3. Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the placeholders:
+
+   - `servers.web` — your server's IP address or hostname.
+   - `proxy.host` — the hostname Fizzy will be served from, e.g.
+     `fizzy.example.com`.
+   - `env.clear.APP_FQDN` — the same hostname. Fizzy uses it to build links in
+     emails, webhooks, and exports.
+   - `env.clear.SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `SMTP_USER_NAME` — your
+     outbound mail server.
+   - `env.clear.MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` — the `From` address for outbound email.
+
+   If your server uses an ARM CPU, change `builder.arch` to `arm64`.
+
+4. Provide your secrets. `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` is read from `config/master.key`
+   automatically. Export your SMTP password before deploying (or edit
+   `.kamal/secrets` to read it from your password manager):
+
+   ```sh
+   export SMTP_PASSWORD=your-smtp-password
+   ```
+
+   The default configuration uses Kamal's built-in local registry, so no
+   external registry account or credentials are required. If you prefer to push
+   images to Docker Hub, update the `registry` section in `config/deploy.yml`
+   and provide `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD`.
+
+5. Deploy:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   If you have Kamal installed globally, `kamal setup` does the same thing.
+
+6. Visit `https://your-hostname` and sign in. The first magic link will be sent
+   through the SMTP server you configured.
+
+## Day-to-day operations
+
+- Deploy changes: `bin/kamal deploy`
+- Open a Rails console: `bin/kamal console`
+- Open a shell in the container: `bin/kamal shell`
+- Tail application logs: `bin/kamal logs`
+- Run a one-off command: `bin/kamal app exec "bin/rails db:migrate"`
+
+Database migrations run automatically on each boot through the container
+entrypoint.
+
+## Data persistence
+
+SQLite databases and uploaded files live under `/rails/storage`, which is
+mounted from the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume. That volume is left untouched
+when a new version is deployed, so your data survives upgrades.
+
+Back up the volume before making big changes, and never remove it unless you
+want to start over:
+
+```sh
+docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data -v "$PWD":/backup alpine \
+  tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage-backup.tar.gz -C /data .
+```
+
+Background jobs run in the same container as the web server, so there is no
+separate worker to deploy or monitor. To give jobs more capacity, set
+`JOB_CONCURRENCY` (or `WEB_CONCURRENCY`) in the `env.clear` section of
+`config/deploy.yml`.
